WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.150
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.470
Today, we are going to read some minds.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:12.230
A few months ago, our first video appeared
on brain machine interfaces.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:17.119
It was about a paper from Neuralink, which
promised a great deal.

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:22.670
For instance, they proposed a brain machine
interface that could read this pig’s thoughts

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.020
as it was running on the treadmill.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.970
And what’s more, they not only read is thoughts,
but they could also predict what the pig’s

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.330
brain is about to do.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:36.540
So this was about reading thoughts related
to movement.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:41.130
And to be able to use these brain machine
interfaces to the fullest, they should be

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:46.600
able to enable some sort of communication
for people who have lost their ability to

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.750
move or speak.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.660
So about writing or speaking?

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:59.620
As impossible as it sounds, can we somehow
restore that, or is that still science fiction?

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.430
Many of you told me that you would like to
hear more about this topic, so due to popular

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:11.400
request, here it is, a look beyond Neuralink’s
project to see what else is out there.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.350
This is a collaboration between Stanford University
and a selection of other institutions, and

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:23.530
it allows brain-to-text transcription, where
all the test subject has to do is imagine

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:28.570
writing the letters, and they magically appear
on the screen.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:34.000
And now, start holding on to your papers,
and just look at how quickly it goes!

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:41.240
90 characters per minute with over 94% of
accuracy, which can be improved to over 99%

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.880
of accuracy with an additional autocorrect
technique.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.750
That is absolutely amazing.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.020
A true miracle.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:55.550
90 characters per minute means that the test
subject here, who has a paralyzed hand, can

00:01:55.550 --> 00:02:02.100
almost think about writing these letters continuously,
and most of them are decoded and put on the

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.350
screen in less than a second.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.819
Also, wait a second, 90 characters per minute?

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:16.270
That is about 80% as fast as the average typing
speed on a smartphone screen for an able-bodied

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.280
person of this age group.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.280
Whoa.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:26.600
It is quite remarkable that even years after
paralysis, the motor cortex is still strong

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:33.610
enough to be read by a brain-computer interface
well enough for such typing speed and accuracy.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:37.770
It truly feels like we are living in a science
fiction world.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:42.230
Of course, not even this technique is perfect,
it has its own limitations.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:48.550
For instance, we can’t edit or delete text,
have no access to capital letters and the

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:54.520
method has a calibration step that takes a
long time, although it doesn’t get significantly

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:58.410
worse if we shorten this calibration time
a bit.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.610
So how does this work?

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:06.950
First, the participant starts thinking of
writing one letter at a time.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:12.060
Here you see the recorded neural activity,
this is subject to decoding.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.590
You can see the decoded signals here.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:21.400
And we can’t just give this to a computer
to distinguish between them as is, we project

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:27.239
these into a 2D latent space where it is easy
to find which letter corresponds to which

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:28.349
region.

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:35.130
Look, they form relatively tight clusters,
therefore it is now easy to decide which of

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.489
the squiggles corresponds to which letter.

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:44.020
The decoding part is done by using a recurrent
neural network, which is endowed with memory

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.530
and can deal with sequences of data.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:53.269
So here, in goes the brain activity, and out
comes the decision that says which character

00:03:53.269 --> 00:03:56.120
these activities correspond to.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:01.230
Of course, our alphabet was not designed to
be decoded with neural networks.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:06.930
So here is an almost science fiction-like
question - how do we reformulate the alphabet

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:13.099
to tailor it to maximize the efficiency of
a neural network decoding our thoughts?

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:18.370
Or simpler, what would the alphabet look like
if neural networks were in charge?

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:23.879
Well, this paper has an answer to that too,
so let’s have a look.

00:04:23.879 --> 00:04:30.330
The squiggles indeed look like they came from
another planet, so what do we gain from this?

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:34.300
Well, look at the distance matrix for the
English alphabet.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:41.020
The diagonal is supposed to be very blue,
but what is not supposed to be blue at all

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.410
are the regions that surround it.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:50.050
Look, the blue color here means that in the
English alphabet, the letters M and N can

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.770
be relatively easily confused.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:57.740
Same with the letters O and C, and there are
many more similarities.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:04.240
And now, look, here is the same distance matrix
for the optimized alphabet.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.610
No dark blue in sight outside the diagonal!

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:10.009
Much easier to process and decode.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:14.740
If neural networks were in charge, this is
what the alphabet would look like.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:15.740
Glorious!

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:21.720
Also, the fact that we are talking about squiggles
is not a trivial insight at all, traditional

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:28.309
methods typically rely on movement in straight
lines to select letters and buttons.

00:05:28.309 --> 00:05:33.699
The other key thought in this paper is that
modern neural network-based methods can decode

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:36.509
these thoughts of squiggles reliably.

00:05:36.509 --> 00:05:38.749
That is absolutely amazing.

00:05:38.749 --> 00:05:45.759
And wait a second…note that there is only
one participant in the user study.

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:50.629
Why just one participant, why not call in
a bunch of people to test this?

00:05:50.629 --> 00:05:57.619
It is because this method uses a microelectrode
array, and this requires surgery to insert,

00:05:57.619 --> 00:06:03.219
and because of that, these studies are difficult
to perform, and are usually done at times

00:06:03.219 --> 00:06:07.399
when the participant has brain surgery anyways
for other reasons.

00:06:07.399 --> 00:06:12.610
Having more people in the study is usually
prohibitively expensive, if at all possible

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.930
for this kind brain implant.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:19.720
And note that research is a process, and these
papers are stepping stones.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:25.800
And now, we are able to help people write
90 characters every minute with a brain machine

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.749
interface, and I can only imagine how good
these techniques will become two more papers

00:06:30.749 --> 00:06:32.199
down the line.

00:06:32.199 --> 00:06:36.229
And don’t forget, there are research works
on non-invasive devices too!

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:38.270
So, what do you think?

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.169
Let me know in the comments below.

00:06:40.169 --> 00:07:31.639
What a time to 
be alive!

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:35.219
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

